-- 1. ARCHITECTURE & KEY CONCEPTS
-- ============================================================================
/*
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ SNOWFLAKE'S 3-LAYER ARCHITECTURE                                            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                             │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │  LAYER 3: CLOUD SERVICES (The Brain)                                  │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  Think of this as the "control plane" — it manages everything but     │  │
│  │  doesn't store your data or run your queries directly.                │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  What it does:                                                        │  │
│  │  • Authentication & Access Control – who can access what              │  │
│  │  • Metadata Management – tracks every table, column, partition        │  │
│  │  • Query Parsing & Optimization – turns SQL into an execution plan    │  │
│  │  • Transaction Management – ensures ACID compliance                   │  │
│  │  • Infrastructure Management – handles all the cloud plumbing         │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  KEY INSIGHT: Cloud services are shared across all customers but      │  │
│  │  fully isolated. You don't pay for this unless it exceeds 10% of     │  │
│  │  your daily compute usage (rare for normal workloads).                │  │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                             │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │  LAYER 2: COMPUTE (Virtual Warehouses - The Muscle)                   │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  Virtual warehouses are INDEPENDENT compute clusters. Each one is     │  │
│  │  like renting a separate "team of workers" that reads from the        │  │
│  │  same shared storage. Multiple warehouses can query the same data     │  │
│  │  simultaneously without competing for resources.                      │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  What it does:                                                        │  │
│  │  • Executes SQL queries, DML, data loading                           │  │
│  │  • Sizes from XS (1 node) to 6XL (512 nodes)                        │  │
│  │  • Scale UP = bigger warehouse for complex queries                   │  │
│  │  • Scale OUT = multi-cluster for many concurrent users               │  │
│  │  • Auto-suspend: stops billing when idle (set to 60s for dev!)       │  │
│  │  • Auto-resume: starts automatically when a query arrives            │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  REAL WORLD EXAMPLE: Your BI dashboard queries use warehouse "BI_WH" │  │
│  │  while a data engineer runs heavy transformations on "ETL_WH".       │  │
│  │  They never slow each other down — complete isolation.               │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  COST: Each size doubles cost. XS=1 credit/hr, S=2, M=4, L=8...    │  │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                             │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │  LAYER 1: STORAGE (Centralized - The Foundation)                      │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  All data is stored in a single, centralized layer using the cloud    │  │
│  │  provider's object storage (S3, Azure Blob, GCS). You never interact │  │
│  │  with this directly — Snowflake manages it entirely.                  │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  How data is stored (MICRO-PARTITIONS):                              │  │
│  │  • Each table is divided into immutable files (50-500MB compressed)  │  │
│  │  • Data stored in columnar format (only reads columns you need)      │  │
│  │  • Automatically compressed (often 4-5x compression ratio)           │  │
│  │  • IMMUTABLE = once written, never modified (enables Time Travel)    │  │
│  │  • Updates create NEW micro-partitions, old ones kept for history    │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  WHY THIS MATTERS:                                                    │  │
│  │  • No vacuuming/compaction needed (unlike Postgres/MySQL)            │  │
│  │  • Time Travel is "free" — old partitions already exist             │  │
│  │  • Zero-copy cloning works because partitions are shared pointers   │  │
│  │                                                                       │  │
│  │  COST: ~$23/TB/month (on-demand) or ~$40/TB pre-paid/cheaper        │  │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                             │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

KEY DIFFERENTIATORS vs. Traditional Data Warehouses:

  1. Separation of Storage & Compute
     Traditional: One server does both storage AND processing (tied together)
     Snowflake:   Storage and compute are independent — scale one without the other
     Example:     Store 100TB but only spin up a Small warehouse for queries

  2. Zero-Copy Cloning
     Traditional: Copying a 1TB database takes 1TB of storage + hours
     Snowflake:   CLONE creates an instant pointer copy — zero extra storage!
     Example:     CREATE TABLE dev_orders CLONE prod_orders; -- instant, free

  3. Semi-Structured Data Support
     Traditional: ETL required to flatten JSON before loading
     Snowflake:   Load JSON/Avro/Parquet as-is into VARIANT columns, query directly
     Example:     SELECT payload:user.name FROM events; -- dot-notation on JSON

  4. Near-Unlimited Concurrency
     Traditional: 50 users on same server → everyone slows down
     Snowflake:   Multi-cluster warehouse auto-scales for concurrent users
     Example:     500 dashboard users trigger auto-scale from 1→10 clusters

  5. No Index Management
     Traditional: DBAs spend hours tuning indexes
     Snowflake:   Automatic micro-partition pruning replaces indexes entirely
     Example:     WHERE order_date = '2025-01-15' → skips irrelevant partitions

  6. Cross-Cloud & Cross-Region Replication
     Snowflake:   Replicate databases between AWS, Azure, GCP regions
     Example:     Keep a read replica in EU for GDPR compliance
*/


