-- 12. DATA SHARING & MARKETPLACE
-- ============================================================================
/*
═══ SECURE DATA SHARING ═══

Snowflake's data sharing is unique: it shares LIVE data without copying.
The consumer account reads directly from the provider's storage layer.

HOW IT WORKS:
  Provider Account                    Consumer Account
  ─────────────────                   ────────────────
  1. Creates a SHARE object           4. Creates a database FROM the share
  2. Grants access to specific        5. Queries shared data with their
     tables/views                        own warehouse (their own compute)
  3. Adds consumer account(s)         6. Always sees CURRENT data (live!)

KEY POINTS:
  • NO data copying — consumer reads provider's storage directly
  • Provider pays for storage, Consumer pays for their own compute
  • Data is always current (no sync delay, no ETL)
  • Provider can revoke access anytime
  • Secure views are required (hide underlying table structure)

═══ MARKETPLACE ═══

  The Snowflake Marketplace has 2000+ free & paid data products:
  • Weather data (for supply chain analytics)
  • Financial data (stock prices, economic indicators)
  • Demographic data (census, consumer behavior)
  • COVID data, geo data, industry benchmarks...

  Getting marketplace data:
  1. Browse marketplace in Snowsight UI
  2. Click "Get" on a listing
  3. A shared database appears in your account instantly
  4. Query it like any other database (it's just a database!)
*/

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Creating a Share (Provider side)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Step 1: Create the share object
CREATE SHARE my_data_share
    COMMENT = 'Sharing daily revenue metrics with partner accounts';

-- Step 2: Grant access to the objects you want to share
GRANT USAGE ON DATABASE my_project_db TO SHARE my_data_share;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA my_project_db.analytics TO SHARE my_data_share;
GRANT SELECT ON TABLE my_project_db.analytics.mv_daily_revenue TO SHARE my_data_share;
-- You can share: tables, secure views, secure UDFs
-- You CANNOT share: regular views (must be SECURE), stages, tasks

-- Step 3: Add consumer accounts
ALTER SHARE my_data_share ADD ACCOUNTS = 'ORG1.CONSUMER_ACCT';

-- Step 4: On the consumer side (they run this):
-- CREATE DATABASE shared_revenue FROM SHARE provider_org.provider_acct.my_data_share;
-- SELECT * FROM shared_revenue.analytics.mv_daily_revenue;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Reader Accounts (for non-Snowflake consumers)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- If your partner doesn't have Snowflake, create a Reader Account for them:
-- They get a limited Snowflake account (you pay for their compute!)
-- CREATE MANAGED ACCOUNT partner_reader ...


-- ============================================================================
-- 13. SECURITY & RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)
-- ============================================================================
/*
═══ RBAC: THE MENTAL MODEL ═══

In Snowflake, ALL access is controlled through ROLES. Users don't get
permissions directly — they get ROLES, and roles have PRIVILEGES.

  USER → has ROLE(s) → ROLE has PRIVILEGES on → OBJECTS

═══ BUILT-IN ROLE HIERARCHY ═══

  ACCOUNTADMIN (top — has EVERYTHING. Like root/sudo. NEVER use daily!)
    ├── SECURITYADMIN (manages users, roles, grants)
    ├── SYSADMIN (manages warehouses, databases, schemas, tables)
    │     ├── YOUR_CUSTOM_ROLE_1 (data_engineer)
    │     │     └── YOUR_CUSTOM_ROLE_2 (data_analyst)
    │     └── YOUR_CUSTOM_ROLE_3 (data_scientist)
    └── USERADMIN (can create users and roles, but not grant on objects)
  PUBLIC (everyone has this automatically — minimal privileges)

BEST PRACTICES:
  • NEVER use ACCOUNTADMIN for daily work (use a custom role)
  • Attach all custom roles under SYSADMIN (so SYSADMIN inherits access)
  • Use FUTURE GRANTS so new objects auto-inherit permissions
  • Principle of least privilege: grant minimum needed access

═══ KEY PRIVILEGE TYPES ═══

  ON DATABASE:  USAGE (can "enter" the database), MONITOR, CREATE SCHEMA
  ON SCHEMA:    USAGE (can "enter" the schema), CREATE TABLE/VIEW/etc.
  ON TABLE:     SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE, REFERENCES
  ON WAREHOUSE: USAGE (can run queries), OPERATE (can resize/suspend)
*/

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Setting Up Custom Roles (Real-World Example)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Create roles for your team
CREATE ROLE data_engineer
    COMMENT = 'Full access to raw/staging schemas, read access to analytics';
CREATE ROLE data_analyst
    COMMENT = 'Read-only access to analytics schema';
CREATE ROLE data_scientist
    COMMENT = 'Read access to all schemas + ability to create temporary tables';

-- Build the hierarchy (custom roles sit under SYSADMIN)
GRANT ROLE data_engineer TO ROLE SYSADMIN;     -- SYSADMIN inherits engineer access
GRANT ROLE data_analyst TO ROLE data_engineer;  -- engineer inherits analyst access
GRANT ROLE data_scientist TO ROLE SYSADMIN;

-- Grant to users
GRANT ROLE data_analyst TO USER aayush_misra;
-- Now user 'aayush_misra' can switch to this role: USE ROLE data_analyst;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Granting Privileges
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Data analyst: read-only access to analytics
GRANT USAGE ON DATABASE my_project_db TO ROLE data_analyst;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA my_project_db.analytics TO ROLE data_analyst;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA my_project_db.analytics TO ROLE data_analyst;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL VIEWS IN SCHEMA my_project_db.analytics TO ROLE data_analyst;

-- FUTURE GRANTS: auto-apply to tables/views created in the future!
GRANT SELECT ON FUTURE TABLES IN SCHEMA my_project_db.analytics TO ROLE data_analyst;
GRANT SELECT ON FUTURE VIEWS IN SCHEMA my_project_db.analytics TO ROLE data_analyst;
-- Without FUTURE GRANTS: every new table needs manual GRANT (easy to forget!)

-- Grant warehouse usage (needed to actually run queries)
GRANT USAGE ON WAREHOUSE analytics_wh TO ROLE data_analyst;

-- Data engineer: full access to raw and staging
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON SCHEMA my_project_db.raw TO ROLE data_engineer;
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON SCHEMA my_project_db.staging TO ROLE data_engineer;
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON FUTURE TABLES IN SCHEMA my_project_db.raw TO ROLE data_engineer;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Data Masking Policy (hide sensitive data from unauthorized roles)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
/*
Masking policies dynamically mask column values based on WHO is querying.
The data_engineer sees full email; the data_analyst sees '***@domain.com'.
*/

CREATE MASKING POLICY mask_email AS (val STRING)
RETURNS STRING ->
    CASE
        WHEN CURRENT_ROLE() IN ('DATA_ENGINEER', 'ACCOUNTADMIN') THEN val
        ELSE REGEXP_REPLACE(val, '.+@', '***@')
        -- 'aayush@company.com' → '***@company.com'
    END;

-- Apply to a column
ALTER TABLE my_project_db.raw.customers
    MODIFY COLUMN email SET MASKING POLICY mask_email;

-- Now:
-- As DATA_ENGINEER: SELECT email FROM customers → 'aayush@company.com'
-- As DATA_ANALYST:  SELECT email FROM customers → '***@company.com'
-- The data is NEVER actually modified — masking happens at query time!

-- Another example: mask phone numbers
CREATE MASKING POLICY mask_phone AS (val STRING)
RETURNS STRING ->
    CASE
        WHEN CURRENT_ROLE() IN ('DATA_ENGINEER', 'ACCOUNTADMIN') THEN val
        ELSE CONCAT('XXX-XXX-', RIGHT(val, 4))
        -- '555-123-4567' → 'XXX-XXX-4567'
    END;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Row Access Policy (row-level security)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
/*
Row Access Policies filter WHICH ROWS a user can see, based on their role
or attributes. Like an invisible WHERE clause applied automatically.
*/

CREATE ROW ACCESS POLICY region_filter AS (region_val VARCHAR)
RETURNS BOOLEAN ->
    CURRENT_ROLE() = 'ACCOUNTADMIN'        -- admins see everything
    OR CURRENT_ROLE() = 'DATA_ENGINEER'    -- engineers see everything
    OR region_val IN (
        SELECT region FROM role_region_mapping
        WHERE role_name = CURRENT_ROLE()
    );
    -- Analysts only see rows for their assigned region

-- Apply to table
ALTER TABLE my_project_db.raw.orders
    ADD ROW ACCESS POLICY region_filter ON (region);
-- Now a West Coast analyst only sees orders WHERE region = 'west'


-- ============================================================================
-- 14. SNOWPARK (Python/Java/Scala in Snowflake)
-- ============================================================================
/*
═══ WHAT IS SNOWPARK? ═══

Snowpark lets you write Python, Java, or Scala code that runs INSIDE
Snowflake's compute layer. Your code gets translated to Snowflake's
execution engine — data never leaves Snowflake!

═══ WHY USE SNOWPARK? ═══

  • Complex transformations that are hard to express in SQL
  • ML feature engineering with Python libraries (pandas, scikit-learn)
  • Custom business logic that requires procedural code
  • Integrate with Python data science ecosystem

═══ KEY CONCEPTS ═══

  DataFrame API:  Like Pandas but lazy — operations build a query plan,
                  execution happens on Snowflake's compute when you collect()
  UDF:            User Defined Function — custom function callable from SQL
  Stored Proc:    Procedural code that can execute multiple SQL statements

═══ WHERE CODE RUNS ═══

  Your Python code → Snowflake translates to execution plan → Runs on
  your warehouse (same compute as SQL) → Results stay in Snowflake

  IMPORTANT: Data NEVER leaves Snowflake. No data transfer to your laptop.
*/

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Python UDF (callable from SQL like a built-in function)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Example: Calculate discount based on product category
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION calculate_discount(price FLOAT, category VARCHAR)
RETURNS FLOAT
LANGUAGE PYTHON
RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.11'
HANDLER = 'calc'
AS $$
def calc(price, category):
    """Apply category-specific discount rates."""
    rates = {
        'electronics': 0.10,   # 10% off electronics
        'clothing': 0.20,      # 20% off clothing
        'food': 0.05,          # 5% off food
        'books': 0.15          # 15% off books
    }
    discount_rate = rates.get(category.lower(), 0.0)
    return round(price * discount_rate, 2)
$$;

-- Use it in SQL just like any built-in function:
SELECT
    product_name,
    price,
    category,
    calculate_discount(price, category) AS discount_amount,
    price - calculate_discount(price, category) AS final_price
FROM my_project_db.raw.products;
-- Runs on the warehouse, processes millions of rows, no data leaves Snowflake!

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Python UDTF (User Defined TABLE Function — returns multiple rows)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Example: Split a comma-separated string into rows
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION split_tags(tags STRING)
RETURNS TABLE(tag STRING, position INT)
LANGUAGE PYTHON
RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.11'
HANDLER = 'SplitTags'
AS $$
class SplitTags:
    def process(self, tags):
        if tags:
            for i, tag in enumerate(tags.split(',')):
                yield (tag.strip(), i)
$$;

-- Use it (TABLE function called with TABLE() syntax):
SELECT order_id, t.tag, t.position
FROM my_project_db.raw.orders,
    TABLE(split_tags(metadata:tags::STRING)) t;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Python Stored Procedure (multi-step data processing)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE process_daily_data()
RETURNS STRING
LANGUAGE PYTHON
RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.11'
PACKAGES = ('snowflake-snowpark-python', 'pandas')
HANDLER = 'main'
AS $$
import snowflake.snowpark as snowpark
from snowflake.snowpark.functions import col, sum as sf_sum, count, avg

def main(session):
    """
    Process raw orders into summary analytics.
    Demonstrates Snowpark DataFrame API.
    """
    # Read source table (lazy — no data loaded yet!)
    orders_df = session.table('my_project_db.raw.orders')

    # Filter and transform (still lazy — building query plan)
    delivered = orders_df.filter(col('status') == 'delivered')

    # Aggregate (like GROUP BY in SQL)
    summary = delivered.group_by('status').agg(
        count('*').alias('total_orders'),
        sf_sum('total_amount').alias('total_revenue'),
        avg('total_amount').alias('avg_order_value')
    )

    # Write results (THIS triggers execution on the warehouse!)
    summary.write.save_as_table(
        'my_project_db.analytics.order_status_summary',
        mode='overwrite'  # truncate + insert
    )

    row_count = summary.count()
    return f'Success! Wrote {row_count} rows to analytics.order_status_summary'
$$;

-- Execute the stored procedure
CALL process_daily_data();
-- Output: 'Success! Wrote 3 rows to analytics.order_status_summary'


-- ============================================================================
-- 15. CORTEX AI & ML FUNCTIONS
-- ============================================================================
/*
═══ SNOWFLAKE CORTEX: AI/ML Without Infrastructure ═══

Cortex provides ready-to-use AI/ML capabilities directly in SQL.
No model training, no GPU setup, no Python environment. Just SQL!

═══ LLM FUNCTIONS (Large Language Models) ═══

  COMPLETE()        — generate text, answer questions, analyze content
  SUMMARIZE()       — condense long text into key points
  TRANSLATE()       — translate between languages
  SENTIMENT()       — score text -1 (negative) to +1 (positive)
  EXTRACT_ANSWER()  — extract specific answers from text passages

═══ ML FUNCTIONS (Traditional ML) ═══

  FORECAST()            — time series prediction (next N periods)
  ANOMALY_DETECTION()   — detect outliers in time series data
  CLASSIFICATION()      — categorize data (train + predict)

═══ VECTOR FUNCTIONS (Semantic/AI Search) ═══

