-- ============================================================================
-- 16. COST MANAGEMENT
-- ============================================================================
/*
═══ UNDERSTANDING SNOWFLAKE COSTS ═══

  COMPONENT         | HOW IT'S CHARGED            | TYPICAL COST
  ──────────────────|─────────────────────────────|──────────────────
  Compute           | Credits × $/credit          | $2-4/credit
  Storage           | TB/month                    | $23/TB (on-demand)
  Data Transfer     | Per GB cross-region         | $0.02-0.05/GB
  Serverless        | Credits (Snowpipe, etc.)    | Varies

  CREDIT COST PER HOUR by warehouse size:
    XS = 1 credit    S = 2 credits    M = 4 credits     L = 8 credits
    XL = 16 credits  2XL = 32         3XL = 64          4XL = 128

  REAL EXAMPLE: A Small warehouse running 8 hours/day for 30 days:
    2 credits/hr × 8 hrs × 30 days = 480 credits × $3/credit = $1,440/month

═══ WHERE MONEY IS WASTED (TOP COST KILLERS) ═══

  1. Warehouses left running idle (forgot AUTO_SUSPEND)
  2. Oversized warehouses (using XL when Small would work)
  3. Redundant queries (no result cache hit due to slight query differences)
  4. Bad queries (full table scans, cartesian joins, spilling to disk)
  5. Over-aggressive clustering on small tables (maintenance cost > benefit)
  6. High Time Travel retention on transient/temp data
*/

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Resource Monitor (budget cap — auto-suspend when limit hit)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

CREATE RESOURCE MONITOR my_monthly_monitor
    WITH CREDIT_QUOTA = 100             -- maximum 100 credits this period
    FREQUENCY = 'MONTHLY'              -- reset every month
    START_TIMESTAMP = IMMEDIATELY       -- start now
    TRIGGERS
        ON 50 PERCENT DO NOTIFY         -- email alert at 50%
        ON 75 PERCENT DO NOTIFY         -- email alert at 75%
        ON 90 PERCENT DO NOTIFY         -- email alert at 90%
        ON 100 PERCENT DO SUSPEND;      -- STOP all queries on this warehouse!
-- SUSPEND_IMMEDIATE also available: kills running queries immediately

-- Attach monitor to a warehouse
ALTER WAREHOUSE analytics_wh SET RESOURCE_MONITOR = my_monthly_monitor;
-- Now analytics_wh will auto-suspend when it hits 100 credits in a month

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Monitor Warehouse Usage (find expensive warehouses)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

SELECT
    WAREHOUSE_NAME,
    DATE_TRUNC('DAY', START_TIME) AS day,
    SUM(CREDITS_USED) AS daily_credits,
    ROUND(SUM(CREDITS_USED) * 3, 2) AS est_daily_cost_usd  -- assuming $3/credit
FROM SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.WAREHOUSE_METERING_HISTORY
WHERE START_TIME >= DATEADD('DAY', -30, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP())
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY daily_credits DESC
LIMIT 20;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Find Expensive Queries (optimize the worst offenders)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

SELECT
    query_id,
    user_name,
    warehouse_name,
    warehouse_size,
    ROUND(total_elapsed_time / 1000, 1) AS seconds,
    ROUND(bytes_scanned / 1024/1024/1024, 2) AS gb_scanned,
    partitions_total,
    partitions_scanned,
    ROUND(credits_used_cloud_services, 4) AS credits_used,
    SUBSTR(query_text, 1, 100) AS query_preview
FROM SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.QUERY_HISTORY
WHERE start_time >= DATEADD('DAY', -7, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP())
  AND total_elapsed_time > 60000  -- queries > 60 seconds
ORDER BY total_elapsed_time DESC
LIMIT 20;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Storage Costs (find large tables to archive/clean)
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

SELECT
    TABLE_CATALOG AS database_name,
    TABLE_SCHEMA AS schema_name,
    TABLE_NAME,
    ROW_COUNT,
    ROUND(BYTES / 1024/1024/1024, 2) AS size_gb,
    ROUND(BYTES / 1024/1024/1024/1024 * 23, 2) AS est_monthly_cost_usd,
    LAST_ALTERED
FROM SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.TABLE_STORAGE_METRICS
WHERE TABLE_CATALOG = 'MY_PROJECT_DB'
  AND DELETED IS NULL
ORDER BY BYTES DESC
LIMIT 20;


-- ============================================================================
-- 17. BEST PRACTICES CHECKLIST
-- ============================================================================
/*
═══ DATA ENGINEERING ═══

  ☐ Use 3-layer architecture: RAW → STAGING → ANALYTICS/CORE
      WHY: Separation of concerns. Raw is your safety net, staging is for
           cleaning, analytics is business-ready. If a transform breaks,
           you still have the original data in RAW.

  ☐ RAW = exact copy of source (no transformation)
      WHY: If source system changes schema, you can re-process from RAW.
      HOW: COPY INTO with minimal transformation, VARIANT for JSON.

  ☐ STAGING = cleaned, typed, deduplicated
      WHY: Catch data quality issues here (before they reach analytics).
      HOW: QUALIFY for dedup, CAST for types, COALESCE for nulls.

  ☐ ANALYTICS = business logic, aggregations, star schema
      WHY: Optimized for BI tools and ad-hoc queries.
      HOW: Dynamic tables or views with clear naming conventions.

  ☐ Use VARIANT for semi-structured data ingestion
      WHY: Load once, figure out schema later. No schema-on-write pain.
      HOW: Load raw JSON into a single VARIANT column, then flatten.

  ☐ Use MERGE for idempotent loads (safe to re-run)
      WHY: If a pipeline fails mid-run, you can re-run without duplicates.
      HOW: MERGE on natural key (order_id, event_id, etc.)

  ☐ Prefer Dynamic Tables over Stream+Task for simple pipelines
      WHY: Less code, automatic incremental refresh, easier maintenance.
      WHEN: Stream+Task is better for complex procedural logic.

  ☐ Use CLONE for dev/test environments (zero storage cost until modified)
      WHY: Full production-like environment for free! No impact on prod.
      HOW: CREATE DATABASE dev_db CLONE prod_db; — instant!

  ☐ Tag tables with metadata (owner, refresh frequency, SLA)
      WHY: Governance at scale. Know who to contact when something breaks.
      HOW: ALTER TABLE SET TAG owner='data_team', sla='1_hour';

═══ PERFORMANCE ═══

  ☐ Filter on clustered/date columns first
      WHY: Enables partition pruning (skip 99% of data).
      EXAMPLE: WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' first, then other filters.

  ☐ Avoid SELECT * in production queries
      WHY: Columnar storage means you only read columns you request.
           SELECT * reads ALL columns → much more data scanned.

  ☐ Use LIMIT during development
      WHY: Don't scan a billion rows to check query logic.

  ☐ Prefer QUALIFY over subqueries for window function filters
      WHY: Cleaner code, same performance, fewer CTEs needed.

  ☐ Use approximate functions for exploration (APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT)
      WHY: 2-5x faster for large tables, <2% error (fine for exploration).

  ☐ Analyze query profile for spillage and cartesian joins
      WHY: Spillage = warehouse too small. Cartesian = bad join condition.
      FIX: Scale up warehouse OR fix the query logic.

═══ SECURITY ═══

  ☐ Never use ACCOUNTADMIN for daily work
      WHY: Accidental DROP DATABASE is permanent with ACCOUNTADMIN.
           Custom roles with limited access = blast radius control.

  ☐ Create functional roles (data_engineer, data_analyst)
      WHY: Roles outlive individual users. When someone leaves, revoke role.

  ☐ Use masking policies for PII
      WHY: Compliance (GDPR, CCPA). Different teams see different things.

  ☐ Grant on FUTURE objects for new tables
      WHY: Without this, every new table needs manual GRANT (will be forgotten!).

  ☐ Enable network policies for IP allowlisting
      WHY: Even with strong passwords, restrict access to known networks.

  ☐ Enable MFA for all users
      WHY: Passwords get leaked. MFA adds a second barrier.

═══ COST ═══

  ☐ AUTO_SUSPEND = 60 for dev, 120 for production
      WHY: Every second a warehouse runs idle = wasted money.
           60s is aggressive (may lose local disk cache), 120s is balanced.

  ☐ Use XS/S warehouses (90% of queries don't need more)
      WHY: Doubling size = doubling cost. Only scale up for proven slow queries.
      TEST: Run the same query on XS and S. If time drops 50%, S is worth it.

  ☐ Set resource monitors on all warehouses
      WHY: Runaway queries or loops can burn thousands of credits overnight.
           Resource monitors are your safety net.

  ☐ Use transient tables for staging/temp data
      WHY: No fail-safe storage = ~25% cheaper. Staging data is re-derivable.

  ☐ Review QUERY_HISTORY weekly for expensive queries
      WHY: Top 10 most expensive queries are often 80% of your bill.
           Optimizing 10 queries can save thousands per month.

  ☐ Consider serverless tasks for infrequent jobs
      WHY: Serverless = pay only for seconds used. No warehouse idling between runs.
           Best for tasks that run < 1 minute every hour.
*/


