═══ COMMON INTERVIEW QUESTIONS & ANSWERS ═══

  Q: "How does Snowflake achieve separation of storage and compute?"
  A: Storage is on cheap cloud object storage (S3/Blob/GCS), shared by all.
     Compute is via independent virtual warehouses that can be started/stopped.
     You scale each independently — big storage + small compute is fine.

  Q: "What happens when you run a query with no warehouse?"
  A: Metadata-only queries work (SHOW, DESCRIBE, COUNT from metadata).
     Everything else fails with "no active warehouse" error.

  Q: "How is zero-copy cloning possible?"
  A: Micro-partitions are immutable. Clone just points to same partitions.
     Only diverges (new storage) when you modify the cloned data.

  Q: "What is the difference between TEMPORARY and TRANSIENT?"
  A: TEMPORARY: visible only in current session, dropped on session end.
     TRANSIENT: visible to all, persists, but no Fail-Safe (cheaper).
     Both: max 1 day Time Travel.

  Q: "How does partition pruning work?"
  A: Each micro-partition stores MIN/MAX values for each column.
     When you filter (WHERE date > X), Snowflake skips partitions where
     MAX(date) < X. This can skip 99% of data if clustered on that column.

  Q: "What's the difference between COPY INTO and INSERT INTO?"
  A: COPY INTO: bulk loading from stages/files. Tracks loaded files (won't
     reload same file). Highly optimized for parallel file loading.
     INSERT INTO...SELECT: standard SQL insert. No file tracking. Uses
     warehouse compute. Fine for table-to-table, bad for file loading.

  Q: "How does Snowpipe work?"
  A: Snowpipe uses serverless compute (not your warehouse) to auto-load
     files as they arrive in a stage. Triggered by cloud event notifications
     (S3 → SNS → Snowpipe) or REST API calls. Pay per-file, not per-hour.

  Q: "Can you explain Snowflake's multi-cluster warehouse?"
  A: It's a POOL of identical warehouses behind a load balancer.
     When concurrent queries exceed capacity, new clusters auto-start.
     Economy mode: waits before scaling (cheaper). Standard: immediate (faster).
     MAX_CLUSTER_COUNT controls the ceiling. Good for BI dashboards with
     many concurrent users.
